[[introduction-crisis]]

== Introduction: The Crisis

Which crisis? I imagine this is what people are asking themselves when they see the title: The Crisis?

Here's just a partial list:
1. Trump
2. MAGA
3. Republican Party
4. Democratic Party
5. AI
6. AI Data Centers
7. Billionaires
8. Political Corruption
9. ICE
10. Trans phobia
11. Iran War
12. Gas prices
13. Diesel prices
14. Climate change
15. Gaza genocide
16. Grocery prices
17. House prices
18. Rental prices
19. Housing availability
20. Anti-vax
30. Islamophobia
31. Anti-immimgration
32. School shootings

I'm sure you can rattle off even more. We have so many crisis. There is one that you are probably not aware of that I can share from personal experience as a software engineer: e-waste. The AI Data Center is a construction problem today, but in a few short years those massive data centers will create massive e-waste. We have no plan.

I mean, I guess, other than transphobia and immigration, we can be grateful the crisis are not our usual culture war issues like abortions and guns.


The crisis in the title harkens back to 1776 and Thomas Paine's, "The Crisis Papers". The crisis then was monarchy. Monarchy was the foundation crisis of all other crisis. This book assumes Old Democracy is the foundation crisis today. This book presents New Democracy as a possible solution.

Unlike Paine's, "The Crisis Papers", there is no need to convince anyone that the current US Government is beyond repair. There exists on the Internet enough information to ascertain our government is beyond repair. This book is for people who already believe our government has to be replaced.

New Democracy is not a government, it is a society. Thomas Jefferson hinted that a fully functional society is the ideal, "The government that governs least, governs best." The positive way to say this is that the society that is fully functional requires no government.

New Democracy is a fully operational society that needs no government.

Even if you believe Old Democracy needs to be replaced, New Democracy may not be for you because it is 100% new.

An adventurer walking into unknown territory faces new risks. New Democracy is an adventure not of land, but of the mind. New Democracy needs adventurers. People who are willing to take risks that may include serious harm.

Why take such risks? Well, revolution is typically the solution for failed governments. Revolution certainly means violence and death. And here's the thing, unless the after revolution plan is to just install the same government with different people, then revolution has no plan.

New Democracy is the plan for after revolution. But here's the kicker, New Democracy does not need a revolution first. New Democracy is designed to dissolve Old Democracy, not destroy it outright. As a fully functioning society takes shape this society will not require any government. This dissolves Old Democracy.

The American Revolution was a revolution with no plan. The US Constitution was only drawn up after the revolution was over. That was a huge risk that shouldn't have been taken. That Constitution should have been available before the first bullet was fire. Completely irresponsible.

New Democracy is responsible. The entire plan is written and ready to go.

So, we can start a revolution with no plan or bypass the pain and misery of revolution and just implement the plan.

Okay, if you've made it this far than you might be asking, what are the actual risks?

Financial. Mostly financial. New Democracy is designed to work in phases. The initial phase is to share financial expenses. A company of size thirty people is started. Thirty is the mesh node size in the mesh design. Then you learn to operate a corporation as a social function. We take advantage of corporate law to share expenses like electricity and such. The ability to share expenses will depend on pooling income and capability to live in close proximity.


Corporations have huge tax and business advantages individuals do not. The first phase is to learn how to live as thirty people under a corporate structure.

Just imagine for a moment if the entire 340 million population were living under corporate structures and not individual structures. No personal income. Everything paid for by the corporation under shared expense, bulk pricing.


The risk here is financial. We have to learn to build trust in society. This trust is the foundation for a society that is fully functional and needs no government.

There is no Constitution, no legal arguments to be had. We are building society. There could be legal needs on day one depending on how much money can be had.

The next phase is to have a corporation campus. Create a corporate campus with housing for thirty people. If the corporation is going to have a business then some office facilities and what not may also be needed. This may require new zoning for work-living arrangements.

Phase one is just form a thirty person campus living anywhere. Share as much expense as possible with smart phone plans and so on.

Phase two is a corporate campus with live-work zoning. Try and get as many people as possible living on campus.

As you can see, phase one can be skipped if enough money exists and just jump to phase two.

Phase three can also be started immediately and requires even more money. Phase three requires building facilities to store a full year's supply of survival needs: food, water, electricity, clothing, and even  healthcare.  Phase three ends the thirty person corporation plan. Once such a corporation has the one year's survival storage then that corporation is considered a mesh node and can now join projects. Projects are phase four. Since there are no mesh nodes in 2026 then phase four cannot be started any time soon.

There is the risk for joining New Democracy today. Starting a corporation and learning to trust thirty people, learning to function as a society.

Notice there are no liberal or conservative requirements. There are no religious requirements. A mesh node is a completely self contained society. As a corporation a mesh node will write their own by-laws, or constitution if you will. The challenge is not which ideology, the challenge is learning to function as a society of thirty people.

New Democracy has a formal name: Irreni. Irreni is an acronym that stands for innovation replaces religion, engineering not ideology. Each mesh node decides on the ideology of choice. New Democracy doesn't get involved. New Democracy is social innovations and social engineering like with social corporations.

The Crisis 20230: Interlude Part 1

(New Democracy is just a few years old in 2030. The first MGO is called MGO Prime. The campus is a live-work campus intended to house the fixed size 30 people of an MGO. A couple of years ago it was just a plot of land with a single, simple four wall conference room. Now the conference room has an adjoining hotel of 30 rooms and a bottom floor conference room.)

Technically MGO Prime has the same thirty people size as all other MGO nodes. However, I always count the setting as part of the crew, so thirty one. Some people count their house AI but to me that's just lazy thinking. AI has no physical presence. A well constructed setting makes or breaks a team. Buildings have a life of their own.

The original building on the campus was just four walls for a conference room to hold thirty people. The center table only seats ten people because most of the time meetings are smaller than ten. There is a semi-circle drive with a corresponding sidewalk for dropping off people. Parking is currently just campus land that hasn't been built yet. The semi-circle island is large enough for an SUV size sign of black granite that says "MGO Prime". The stone is deliberately sunk at a thirty degree angle. The theme is unnerving and unsettling so the sign being at a thirty-degree slope rather than level is suppose to represent that. Personally, I think it's just there to gaslight people with various OCD tendencies. Like I said, setting has a life of its own.

The conference room is a simple cube. The roof being flat is a problem in Maine. One of use has roof snow removal duty. Although, since the building heats the roof it's not as bad as you think. Plus, there are solar panels on the root that needs cleaning The flat roof makes that possible.

Three of the four walls have identical pairs of bay windows that slide open horizontally. They are big enough to count has fire exits. The windows are at waist level and right below them are removable shelves that at as open workspace areas. They are wide enough to put a chair under. On those occasions all thirty of us are in the room, then the shelves can act as benches to sit on, 10 people on each side. That arrangement can get weird though.

The two bay windows have enough space between them to mount LED monitors on swivel mounts. Each window houses four monitors. The way this works is virtual meeting members each get a monitor rather than using typical laptop software that can show people. Each of the sixteen monitors have corresponding cameras attached such that virtual meeting attendees feel like they are in the room viewing the room at their placement. Mybrid wrote software to reserve a monitor position when attending the meeting. In all there can be thirty people on site and 16 people virtually in meetings. Although mostly the MGO team members use the remote monitors because only about half of our team lives on campus at the moment.

There is one other unusual feature about the virtual meeting setup. There is a camera dedicated to turning remote volume on and off. People on site just need to raise their hand and remote audio for all sixteen guests goes off. Lowering your hand turns the audio back on. It is a really hand feature. Ha! Get it?


The wall facing the street is where the speak presents. If needed, blackout screens can be lowered over the front bay windows and act as project screens. The other windows just have standard blackout curtains. If the blackout screens aren't down then you can see who is presenting as you drive up to drop people off. This is very useful for dodging a meeting. The building talks to you if you let it. The building is quite helpful.

Today, everyone is attending and there are no virtual members. MGO only meeting.


There is a crisis, a nuclear threat has been made against the MGO Prime data center.













